.TH ECHO 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
echo \- write arguments to standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B echo
.RB [ \-n ]
.RI [ string ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B echo
writes its arguments to standard output, followed by a
.RB < newline >.
If there are no arguments, only the
.RB < newline >
is written.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Suppress the trailing
.RB < newline >.
Recognized only as the very first argument; a
.B \-n
appearing anywhere else is written out like any other
.I string
operand.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I string
A string to be written to standard output, verbatim.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
Backslash sequences
.RI ( \e n ,
.IR \e t ,
and so on) are
.B never
interpreted by this implementation, in any mode \(em a literal
.RI \(dq \e n \(dq
in an argument prints as the two characters backslash-n, never a
newline. A script wanting byte-for-byte portable escape handling
should use
.BR printf (1)
instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B >0
A write to standard output failed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR printf (1),
.BR cat (1)
